REGIONAL REGULATION OF ENREKANG REGENCY

NUMBER 1 OF 2018

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF ENREKANG.

Considering:

a. that in order to guarantee and protect the rights of women and children so that they can participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and honor, and receive protection from violence, it is necessary to undertake protection efforts for women and children;
b. that violence against women and children in Enrekang Regency continues to increase and spread, causing community members to feel unsafe in carrying out their lives, thus requiring integrated protection efforts;
c. that based on the provisions in Article 12 of Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government, women's empowerment and child protection are mandatory governmental affairs for regional governments;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 29 of 1959 concerning Establishment of Level II Regions in Sulawesi (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 74, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1822);
3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019);
4. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
5. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
6. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
7. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
8. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
9. Law Number 20 of 2003 concerning National Education System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 78, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4301);
10. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
11. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
12. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
13. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
14. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
15. Presidential Regulation Number 18 of 2014 concerning Protection of Women and Children in Social Conflict (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 44);
16. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 6 of 2015 concerning System for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 615);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF ENREKANG REGENCY

and

REGENT OF ENREKANG

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE

Article 2

The implementation of Protection of Women and Children is based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination;
b. the best interests of women and children;
c. the right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the views of children;
e. legal certainty;
f. local wisdom;
g. justice and gender equality; and
h. sustainability.

Article 3

The implementation of Protection of Women and Children aims to:
a. provide guidance to the regional government in planning, policies and strategies for the protection of women and children;
b. strengthen the protective environment for women and children from all forms of violence, exploitation, mistreatment, and neglect;
c. prevent violations of the rights of women and children;
d. increase empowerment of women and children; and
e. increase the role of government institutions, business entities, media and/or Non-Governmental Organizations in the region.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of Protection of Women and Children includes:
a. prevention;
b. implementation of protection of women and children;
c. services for women and children victims of violence;
d. institutions;
e. cooperation and partnership; and
e. guidance and supervision.

CHAPTER III RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Part One Rights of women

Article 5

Every woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from discriminatory actions;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, neglect and Trafficking;
d. obtain decent work in accordance with her abilities;
e. obtain work leave while continuing to receive wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education; and
g. receive special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health in connection with women's reproductive function.

Part Two Rights of children

Article 6

Every child has the right to:
a. live, grow, develop and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity;
b. obtain protection from discriminatory actions;
c. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
d. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, neglect and Trafficking;
e. a name as personal identity and citizenship status;
f. obtain a birth certificate free of charge;
g. embrace religion and worship according to the teachings of their religion with the guidance of parents/guardians;
h. not be forced to change religion by foster parents, adoptive parents and by anyone;
i. be recognized and know their parents, be raised and cared for by their own parents;
j. obtain health services and social security in accordance with physical, mental, spiritual and social needs;
k. obtain education and teaching in accordance with their interests and talents;
1. obtain care, education and training, improve themselves and the ability to participate in community and state life for children with special needs;
m. express and have their opinions heard; and
n. rest and utilize leisure time for self-development.

Article 7

1. The Regional Government in efforts to protect women and children has the obligation and responsibility to:
a. guarantee the rights of women and children without discrimination in accordance with statutory regulations;
b. establish and implement policies, programs and activities for the protection of the rights of women and the rights of children;
c. provide support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of protection for women and children;
d. cooperate with the community and/or community organizations in the implementation of protection for women and children in accordance with statutory regulations;
e. carry out rehabilitation of women and children who are victims of violence;
f. provide special protection to women victims of violence and trafficking in women and children;
g. provide special protection to children in emergency situations, children in conflict with the law, children victims of violence and trafficking in women and children, children from minority and isolated groups, children exploited economically and/or sexually, children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic abuse and children with HIV/AIDS;
h. organize inclusive education for children with Disabilities; and
i. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by PD in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 10

1. Parents/guardians in efforts to protect the rights of children have the obligation and responsibility to:
a. care for, maintain, educate and protect children;
b. develop children according to their abilities, talents and interests;
c. provide formal and non-formal education;
d. guide children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and
e. prevent marriage at child age.
2. In the event that parents/guardians do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may transfer to the family.

CHAPTER V PREVENTION

Part one Regional Government

Article 12

The Regional Government carries out prevention of violations of the rights of women and children by:
a. conducting socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women and children;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against women and children;
c. developing child-friendly schools;
d. developing child-friendly health centers;
e. encouraging increased participation of children through the establishment of children's forums;
f. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women and children;
g. developing family empowerment programs;
h. realizing the development of Child-Friendly Regency, District, and Village; and
i. creating Child-Friendly Open Public Spaces (RPTRA).

Article 13

The community carries out prevention of violations of the rights of women and children by:
a. monitoring various conditions that occur in their environment that may cause violations of the rights of women and children;
b. strengthening the role of community organizations, religious organizations and the family environment;
c. preventing unregistered marriages and siri marriages;
d. preventing marriage at Child age; and
e. strengthening community participation in recovery and social reintegration.

Article 36

Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal assistance, and
e. repatriation and social reintegration.

Part Two Complaint Services

Article 37

1. The implementation of complaint services as referred to in Article 36 letter a is carried out through integrated services.
2. Integrated services for women and children victims of violence use the principles of:
a. fast, responsive, safe, empathetic;
b. gender responsive;
c. non-discriminatory;
d. easily accessible;
e. guarantee of confidentiality, and
f. without charges to the victim.

Article 39

Complaint handling services for victims of violence against women and children are carried out by:
a. Hospitals;
b. Community Health Centers;
c. P2TP2A;
d. Police;
e. Office of Religious Affairs;
f. Legal Aid Institutions;
g. Child Protection Institutions;
h. Family Welfare Consultation Institutions;
i. PPA Task Force

Article 46

1. Legal aid as referred to in Article 36 letter d is aimed at providing assistance and protection to women and children victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and certainty of their legal rights.
2. Types of legal aid services include:
a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of Investigation Reports;
b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the Prosecutor's Office;
c. legal aid in the examination process at the Court;
d. legal aid in the court decision process until there is a decision with permanent legal force;
e. legal aid in the mediation process;
f. legal aid in the Diversion process.
3. The Regional Government provides legal aid to children and women victims of violence.
4. In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with Legal Aid Institutions, Advocates and/or other gender-responsive legal assistants.

Part Two

Article 50

1. LK3 services as referred to in Article 48 paragraph (2) letter b, constitute services to families experiencing psychosocial problems.
2. Types of LK3 services as referred to in paragraph (1), consist of:
a. consultation;
b. counseling;
c. information;
d. advocacy;
e. referral; and
f. outreach.
3. The membership composition of LK3 is stipulated by Regent Decree.

Article 52

1. In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of Women and Children Protection, the Regional Government establishes a Task Force.
3. The Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duty to assist the Regional Apparatus that handles women's empowerment and child protection in addressing women's and children's issues.
4. In carrying out the duties as referred to in paragraph (1), the task force has the following functions:
a. conducting outreach to women and children experiencing problems;
b. identifying the conditions and services needed by women and children experiencing problems;
c. protecting women and children at the scene from things that may endanger them;
d. placing and evacuating women and children experiencing problems;
e. making recommendations to women's and children's service organizations established by the region to obtain further services.
5. The task force as referred to in paragraph (1) is under the coordination of the Regional Apparatus that administers affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
6. The establishment of the Task Force shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.
7. Further provisions regarding organizational structure, procedures for establishment and work mechanisms shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 53

1. In order to achieve the objectives of Women and Children Protection, cooperation may be conducted with:
a. the central government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments; and
d. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. delivery of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses.

Article 54

1. The Regional Government may establish partnerships with the business sector in the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
2. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted through:
a. employment opportunity information system for women victims of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of violence;
c. educational assistance for women and children victims of violence who have been withdrawn from their education; and
d. fostering and enhancing the economic independence of women victims of violence;
3. Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Cooperation Agreement.

Article 55

1. The Regional Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of Women and Children Protection carried out by the community and community institutions.
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.
3. Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted with the principles of:
a. professional;
b. transparent; and
c. accountable.

Article 56

1. Coordination of Regional Apparatus includes:
a. health service referrals to victims;
b. legal aid;
c. repatriation of victims of trafficking in women and children;
d. social reintegration; and
e. development of forms of Women and Children Protection.
2. Development of forms of protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e includes:
a. mediation process for Domestic Violence cases;
b. assistance for people with HIV/AIDS (ODHA) and people living with HIV/AIDS (OHIDHA);
c. assistance for victims of human trafficking;
d. child victims and sexual exploitation or other exploitation; and
e. other issues directly related to efforts to protect women and children.

CHAPTER XII

COMMUNITY AND PRIVATE SECTOR PARTICIPATION

Article 59

1. The community and private sector participate in assisting efforts to protect women and children from all forms of violence, exploitation and discrimination.
2. Forms of community and private sector participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may include:
a. establishing family partners at the Kelurahan/Village level by the community;
b. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
c. reporting or providing information on incidents of violence, exploitation and discrimination experienced by women and children;
d. providing safe houses and shelters;
e. providing legal aid for children in conflict with the law;
f. providing educational scholarships;
g. providing playgrounds for children;
h. conducting supervision of the implementation of women's and children's protection; and
i. other participation relevant to child protection efforts.
